{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["The sense of <b>and YHWH knows those who are His</b> — for the service of Levi, <b>and the holy one</b> — for the priesthood. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Numbers 16:5), and he explained it well. The verse says that HaShem will make known whether the Levites are His, as it is said, \"and the Levites shall be Mine\" (Numbers 8:14), or whether the firstborn are still His, as it is said, \"for every firstborn is Mine\" (Numbers 8:16), that He has not exchanged them for the Levites. \"And the holy one\" — these are the priests consecrated for the service, as it is said, \"and Aaron was set apart to consecrate him as most holy\" (I Chronicles 23:13). For Korah, in order to gather the people to himself, would also stir division among the Levites, and he wished to return the entire service to the firstborn. Now when these firstborn were consumed by fire, it became known that HaShem had not chosen their service, and when Aaron's incense was accepted, it became known that he is the holy one." "The sense of <b>and him whom He chooses, He will bring near to Him</b> a second time, is that HaShem will make known those who are His and the holy one, and will bring him near to Him tomorrow and turn to his offering, while to the others and to their offering He will not turn. And him whom He chooses for all generations, to stand and minister before Him, he and his seed forever — \"He will bring near to Him tomorrow,\" meaning that this test will be for them a sign for all generations never to quarrel, neither against the chosen one nor against his seed forever.\n\nAnd Rabbeinu Chananel wrote that all these assembled groups were Levites from the tribe of Korah, and this is the reason for \"You take too much upon yourselves, sons of Levi\" (Numbers 16:7), \"Hear now, sons of Levi\" (Numbers 16:8). Perhaps they thought that their entire tribe was chosen for the priesthood and that Moshe of his own accord bestowed honor upon his brother — end quote. But Heaven forbid that in the tribe of the ministers of our Elohim there should be two hundred and fifty called men, princes, men of rebellion, kicking against their master and against the great one of their tribe, and complaining against HaShem! And had they been from that tribe alone, not all the tribes of Israel would have complained on the morrow, saying, \"You have killed the people of YHWH\" (Numbers 17:6), for none of them had died except from the tribe of Moshe and Aaron. Also the sign of the staffs is proof that the division involved all the tribes of Israel. And Scripture specified \"and men of the children of Israel\" (Numbers 16:2) to indicate that they were from all the tribes, not from the two mentioned ones alone. Rather, the reason for \"You take too much upon yourselves, sons of Levi\" is because Korah was enticing all the tribes, claiming he sought to restore the service to their firstborn for their honor, as he said, \"for all the congregation, all of them, are holy\" (Numbers 16:3). And Moshe, in his wisdom, revealed to all the people what was hidden in his heart — that Korah was crying out about his priesthood — and said that the honor of his tribe was sufficient for him: \"You take too much upon yourselves, sons of Levi.\" Then he went back and said, \"Hear now, sons of Levi,\" addressing Korah, their great one, and this is the sense of \"and Moshe said to Korah\" (Numbers 16:8) — but he included his words with the sons of Levi, for Moshe's words in his wisdom were an appeasement to him and to the entire tribe, so that no one from among them would be drawn after him.\n\nNow Moshe himself devised this plan and desired the incense offering more than all other sacrifices, for he had already seen with Nadav and Avihu that when they offered alien incense before YHWH they were consumed, and he permitted Aaron to offer it for the need of the hour — or perhaps it was the morning incense that he would offer when he kindled the lamps as prescribed. And Moshe was confident that HaShem would fulfill the word of His servant and complete the counsel of His messengers.\n\nSome say that \"Moshe heard and fell on his face\" was in order to inquire of HaShem to know what to do, and then it was said to him, \"In the morning YHWH will make known,\" etc., but this was not mentioned except in Moshe's account to the people. And I have already shown you that in many places Scripture sometimes elaborates in the speech of HaShem to Moshe and abbreviates in Moshe's retelling, and sometimes does the reverse, and sometimes does not mention one of them at all — as in the matter of the children of Gad and the children of Reuben, where Scripture recounted the event through Moshe himself, though it was done at the word of HaShem, as they said: \"Whatever HaShem has spoken to your servants, so we shall do\" (Numbers 32:31), and it is written in Joshua (22:9): \"to the land of Gilead, to the land of their possession which they held, at the word of YHWH through the hand of Moshe.\" And if we say that it was so because of HaShem's approval, as it says, \"This is the land that you shall inherit by lot, which HaShem commanded to give to the nine tribes and a half\" (Numbers 34:13), then this would be among the things that Moshe did on his own understanding and the Holy One, blessed be He, agreed with him (Shabbat 87a) — but it is not right that Moshe would do anything regarding the division of the land without authority, for everything is done at the command of HaShem, as it is written, \"To these you shall divide the land,\" etc.\n\nAnd my view on this matter and on what he said to Aaron, \"Take the firepan and put incense\" (Numbers 17:11), is that the hand of HaShem was upon them, and this is what is called ruach ha-kodesh (the holy spirit), as in the books of David and Solomon who acted through ruach ha-kodesh, and as it says, \"The spirit of HaShem spoke through me, and His word was on my tongue\" (II Samuel 23:2). For Moshe our master is trusted in all His house, as I have explained the meaning of \"the house\" (Exodus 33:19), and as I have recalled many times. And because this was not part of the normal mode of Moshe's prophecy, the word of HaShem was not mentioned in these instances.\n\nAnd I saw in Onkelos that he translated every mention of incense in this passage as \"ketorta\" in his language, and did not say of it \"ketorta de-busmin\" (incense of spices) as is his custom. It appears that he held it was not the incense of spices of the Sanctuary, but frankincense and the like, which he offered as a test. And so it was with the sons of Aaron (Leviticus 10:1). But the view of our Rabbis is that it was the incense of the Sanctuary, and that is correct. Or perhaps Onkelos did not wish to dignify the incense by adding \"spices\" to it, since its matter was done in the context of a command."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:06:26.005935926Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}